\section{Introduction}

Boolean algebras and Heyting algebras are some of the most studied classes of algebras in algebraic logic. It is well known that both form a variety, thus allowing the use of powerful tools from 
universal algebra in their study. The situation changes when we restrict our attention to the categories $\CBA$ of complete boolean algebras with complete boolean morphisms and $\CHA$ of complete Heyting algebras with complete Heyting morphisms. %between them. 
Indeed, it is a classic result from the 1960s that the free complete boolean algebra on countably many generators does not exist \cite{Gai64,Hal64}. De Jongh \cite{DeJ80} proved that already the two-generated free complete Heyting algebra does not exist (see also \cite{BK24}). Thus, neither $\CBA$ nor $\CHA$ is 
a variety. We recall that a variety is a class of algebras closed under homomorphic images, subalgebras, and products. Natural generalizations of varieties are quasi-varieties (classes of algebras closed under isomorphisms, subalgebras, products, and ultraproducts), which further generalize to prevarieties (classes of algebras closed under isomorphisms, subalgebras, and products). 
It is well known that each prevariety viewed as a category is complete and cocomplete (see, e.g., 
\cite[Thm.~IV.2.1.3 and IV.2.2.3]{SR99}). Therefore, each category that is not cocomplete is not equivalent to a prevariety. 
Consequently, neither $\CBA$ nor $\CHA$ is equivalent to even a prevariety.  


There are other natural morphisms to consider between complete boolean algebras and complete Heyting algebras. For example, we can look at the category 
of complete boolean algebras with boolean morphisms between them, as well as the category 
of complete Heyting algebras with Heyting morphisms, 
or, more generally, bounded lattice morphisms between them. We show that neither of these categories is equivalent to a prevariety, 
and further generalize this to prove that the category of Heyting algebras with bounded lattice morphisms between them is also not equivalent to a prevariety.  

In addition, we look at BAOs (boolean algebras with an operator), and show that a similar phenomenon occurs there. The morphisms we consider are {\em stable morphisms} (that is, boolean morphisms $f \colon A\to B$ satisfying $\Diamond f(a) \le f(\Diamond a)$ for each $a\in A$), which play an important role in the study of 
the finite model property in modal logic (\cite{Ghi10,BBI16}). Such morphisms 
also play an important role in the study of {\em The Algebra of Topology} of McKinsey and Tarski (\cite{MT44,BR23}). In fact, our article is motivated by the question of Peter Jipsen, who asked, at the \href{https://sites.google.com/view/frametheoryseminar/frame-theory-workshop?authuser=0}{Frame Theory Workshop} (Chapman University, December 2024), whether the category $\MT$ of McKinsey-Tarski algebras is (equivalent to) a variety. We will answer this question in the negative by showing that $\MT$ is not equivalent even to a prevariety. In fact, we show that neither the category of McKinsey-Tarski algebras (MT-algebras for short) with stable morphisms nor that with complete stable morphisms is equivalent to a prevariety. These results generalize to the categories of complete BAOs with these two types of morphisms, as well as to the categories of BAOs with stable morphisms. 

We briefly describe the flavor of our results. In \cref{sec: CBAOs} we show that various categories of complete BAOs with complete stable morphisms lack some countable copowers. In \cref{sec: cBA} we prove that dropping the completeness assumption from the above notion of morphism results in categories that lack even some binary copowers. In \cref{sec: 3.2} we show that dropping the completeness assumption also from the objects results in categories that lack some coequalizers. 
Finally, in \cref{sec: 5} we prove that the category of frames with Heyting morphisms lacks some binary copowers, and so does the category of frames with bounded lattice morphisms. Furthermore, we prove that the category of Heyting algebras with bounded lattice morphisms lacks some coequalizers.

In \cref{sec: cBA,sec: 3.2,sec: 5} our main tool is duality theory: Stone duality for boolean algebras, J\'onsson-Tarski duality for BAOs, and  Esakia and Priestley dualities for Heyting algebras and bounded distributive lattices. Dualizing the fact that prevarieties are cocomplete, it is then sufficient to show that the dual categories under question are not complete. This we do by showing that in these categories either products or equalizers do not exist.


\section{Categories of complete BAOs with complete stable morphisms}\label{sec: CBAOs}


In this section we show that various categories of complete BAOs with complete stable morphisms are not cocomplete, and hence are not equivalent to a prevariety. This, in particular, implies that the category of MT-algebras and MT-morphisms is not equivalent to a variety, thus resolving the question of Peter Jipsen in the negative. 

We recall \cite{JT51} that an {\em operator} on a boolean algebra $B$ is a unary function $\Diamond \colon B \to B$ preserving finite joins, and that the pair $(B,\Diamond)$ is called a {\em boolean algebra with an operator} or simply a {\em BAO}. 
A {\em BAO-morphism} between two BAOs $A$ and $B$ is a boolean morphism $f \colon A \to B$ such that $f(\Diamond a)=\Diamond f(a)$ for each $a\in A$. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $\bf BAO$ denote the category of BAOs and BAO-morphisms.
\end{definition}

We will mainly be interested in {\em stable} morphisms between BAOs \cite{BBI16}; that is, boolean morphisms $f \colon A \to B$ such that $\Diamond f(a) \le f(\Diamond a)$ for each $a\in A$. Stable morphisms are also known as {\em continuous morphisms} and play an important role in the study of axiomatization, finite model property, and decidability of modal logics \cite{Ghi10}. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $\bf BAO_{st}$ denote the category of BAOs with stable morphisms between them.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    Clearly $\bf BAO$ is a wide subcategory of $\BAO$.
\end{remark}

The following classes of BAOs play a prominent role in modal logic (see, e.g., \cite{CZ97}): 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A {\em {\bf T}-algebra} is a BAO $(B,\Diamond)$ such that $a \le \Diamond a$ for each $a \in B$. 
    \item A {\em {\bf K4}-algebra} is a BAO $(B,\Diamond)$ such that $\Diamond\Diamond a \le \Diamond a$ for each $a \in B$.
    \item A {\em {\bf B}-algebra} is a BAO $(B,\Diamond)$ such that $\Diamond\neg\Diamond a \le\neg a$ for each $a \in B$.
    \item A {\em {\bf TB}-algebra} is a BAO which is both a {\bf T}-algebra and a {\bf B}-algebra.
    \item A {\em closure algebra} is a BAO which is both a {\bf T}-algebra and a {\bf K4}-algebra. 
    \item A {\em monadic algebra} is a BAO which is both a closure algebra and a {\bf B}-algebra.
\end{enumerate}

As the name suggests, {\bf T}-algebras serve as algebraic models of the modal system $\bf T$, and the same applies to $\bf K4$-algebras, $\bf B$-algebras, and $\bf TB$-algebras. Closure algebras were introduced by McKinsey and Tarski \cite{MT44} and they serve as algebraic models of $\bf S4$, and monadic algebras by Halmos \cite{Hal56} and they serve as algebraic models of $\bf S5$. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $\T$ denote the full subcategory of $\BAO$ consisting of $\bf T$-algebras, and define $\Kfour$, $\B$, $\TB$, $\CA$, and $\MA$ similarly.    
\end{definition}

We have the following inclusion relationships between these categories:


\[\begin{tikzcd}[sep=small, column sep=tiny]
	&& \BAO \\
	\\
	\Kfour && \T && \B \\
	\\
	& \CA && \TB \\
	\\
	&& \MA
	\arrow[hook, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=3-3, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook', from=3-5, to=1-3]
	\arrow[hook', from=5-2, to=3-1]
	\arrow[hook, from=5-2, to=3-3]
	\arrow[hook', from=5-4, to=3-3]
	\arrow[hook, from=5-4, to=3-5]
	\arrow[hook', from=7-3, to=5-2]
	\arrow[hook, from=7-3, to=5-4]
\end{tikzcd}\]

Complete closure algebras were named McKinsey-Tarski algebras in \cite{BR23}. They provide an alternate pointfree approach to topology.

\begin{definition}
    A {\em McKinsey-Tarski algebra}, or simply an {\em MT-algebra}, is a complete closure algebra. An {\em MT-morphism} between MT-algebras is a complete boolean morphism that is stable. Let $\MT$ denote the category of MT-algebras and MT-morphisms. 
\end{definition}

Furthermore, we let $\CBAO$ denote the category of complete BAOs with complete stable morphisms between them, and define $\CT$, $\CKfour$, $\CB$, $\CTB$, and $\CMA$ similarly. 

\begin{definition}
    Let $\bf BA$ denote the category of boolean algebras with boolean morphisms, and $\CBA$ the category of complete boolean algebras with complete boolean morphisms.
\end{definition}

We will use the following well-known result from the 1960s:

\begin{theorem}[\cite{Gai64,Hal64}] \label{thm: Gai Hal}
     The free countably generated complete boolean algebra does not exist. 
     Thus,
     $\CBA$ is not cocomplete, and hence is not equivalent to a prevariety.
\end{theorem}

We prove that the forgetful functor $\mathcal{U} \colon \CBAO \to \CBA$ has both left and right adjoints. For each boolean algebra $B$, consider two operators $\Diamond_0,\Diamond_s \colon B \to B$, given by $\Diamond_0 a = 0$ and
\[
\Diamond_s a=
\begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } a = 0\\
    1 & \text{if } a \ne 0
\end{cases}
\]
for each $a \in B$.
It is straightforward to see that $(B,\Diamond_0)$ is a $\bf K4$-algebra and a $\bf B$-algebra, and that $(B,\Diamond_s)$ is a monadic algebra.
The operator $\Diamond_s$ is usually referred to as a {\em simple} operator (since $(B,\Diamond_s)$ is a simple algebra \cite{Hal56}).


\begin{theorem} \plabel{t:adjoints}
\hfill\begin{enumerate}
        \item \label[t:adjoints]{i:U-CBAO-adjoint}
        Let $\C$ be $\CBAO,\CB,$ or $\CKfour$. 
        The forgetful functor $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ is both a left and right adjoint.
        \item \label[t:adjoints]{i:U-MT-adjoint}
        Let $\C$ be $\CT,\CTB,\MT,$ or $\CMA$.
        The forgetful functor $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ is both a left and right adjoint.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    \eqref{i:U-CBAO-adjoint}. We define the functor $\mathcal{L} \colon \CBA \to \C$ as follows. On objects, $\mathcal{L}(A) = (A, \Diamond_s)$; and on morphisms, $\mathcal{L}$ is the identity. Since $\mathcal{L}(A)$ is a monadic algebra, it is in particular a $\bf K4$-algebra and a $\bf B$-algebra, and hence $\mathcal L$ is well defined.
    Also, define the functor $\mathcal{R} \colon \CBA \to \C$ as follows. On objects,
    $\mathcal{R}(A) = (A, \Diamond_0)$; and on morphisms, $\mathcal{R}$ is the identity. Since $\mathcal R(A)$ is a $\bf K4$-algebra and a $\bf B$-algebra, $\mathcal R$ is well defined. 

    For $A \in \CBA$ and $B \in \C$ we have 
    \[
    \hom_{\C}(\mathcal{L} (A),B) \cong \hom_{\CBA}(A,\mathcal{U}(B)) \]
    and
    \[
    \hom_{\C}(B, \mathcal{R} (A)) \cong \hom_{\CBA}(\mathcal{U}(B),A),
    \]
    where both bijections are identities.
    We first verify the first identity.
    The inclusion $\subseteq$ is clear since each $\C$-morphism is a complete boolean morphism.
    For the reverse inclusion, suppose $f \colon A \to \mathcal{U}(B)$ is a complete boolean morphism. We claim that it is a stable morphism from $\mathcal{L}(A)$ to $B$, i.e.\ that $\Diamond f(a) \leq f(\Diamond_s a)$.
    If $a = 0$, then 
    \[
    \Diamond f(0) = \Diamond 0 = 0 = f(0) = f(\Diamond_s 0).
    \]
    If $a \neq 0$, then 
    \[
    \Diamond f(a) \leq 1 = f(1) = f(\Diamond_s a).
    \]
    Thus, $f$ is a $\C$-morphism.

    For the second identity, the inclusion $\subseteq$ is clear since each $\C$-morphism is a complete boolean morphism.
    For the reverse inclusion, suppose $f' \colon \mathcal{U}(B) \to A$ is a $\CBA$-morphism.
    Then it is a stable morphism from $B$ to $\mathcal{R}(A)$ because, for every $a \in B$, 
    \[
    \Diamond_0 f'(a) = 0 \leq f'(\Diamond a),
    \]
    yielding that $f'$ is a $\C$-morphism.

    \eqref{i:U-MT-adjoint}.
    To prove that the forgetful functor $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ has a left adjoint, it is enough to observe that, for every $A \in \CBA$, the object $\mathcal{L}(A)$ defined in the above proof is a monadic algebra.

    To prove that $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ has a right adjoint, we define the functor $\mathcal{R}' \colon \CBA \to \C$ as follows.
    On objects,
    $\mathcal{R}'(A) = (A, \Diamond_i)$, where $\Diamond_i$ is the identity on $B$; and on morphisms, $\mathcal{R}'$ is the identity. Since $(B,\Diamond_i)$ is a monadic algebra, $\mathcal R'$ is well defined. 
    We prove that, for $A \in \CBA$ and $B \in \C$, we have
    \[
    \hom_{\C}(B, \mathcal{R}'(A)) = \hom_{\CBA}(\mathcal{U}(B),A).
    \]
    The inclusion $\subseteq$ is clear since each $\C$-morphism is a complete boolean morphism.
    For the reverse inclusion, suppose $f' \colon \mathcal{U}(B) \to A$ is a $\CBA$-morphism.
    Then $a \leq \Diamond a$ for every $a \in B$ because $(B,\Diamond)$ is a $\bf T$-algebra. Therefore, $f'$ is a stable morphism from $B$ to $\mathcal{R}'(A)$ because, for every $a \in B$, we have $\Diamond_i f'(a) = f'(a) \leq f'(\Diamond a)$. Thus, $f$ is a $\C$-morphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem} \label{thm: MT not cocomplete}
    Let $\C$ be a category and $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ a colimit-preserving functor.
    If there is $X \in \C$ such that $\mathcal{U}(X)$ is the four-element boolean algebra, then $\C$ lacks some countable copowers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
    Suppose all countable copowers exist in $\C$. 
    Since $\mathcal{U} \colon \C \to \CBA$ preserves colimits, there exists a countably-indexed copower $C$ of $\mathcal{U}(X)$ in $\CBA$.
    Because $\BA$ and $\CBA$ share finitely generated free objects (see, e.g., \cite[p.~33]{Joh82}), the four-element boolean algebra $\mathcal{U}(X)$ is the free object on one generator in $\CBA$, 
    and so $C$ is the free object on $\omega$ generators in $\CBA$, contradicting \cref{thm: Gai Hal}.
\end{proof}


Since the four-element boolean algebra equipped with $\Diamond_s$ belongs to each of the categories in the theorem below, as an immediate consequence of \cref{t:adjoints,thm: MT not cocomplete} we obtain: 

\begin{theorem}
    Each of the categories $\CBAO$, $\CT$, $\CKfour$, $\CB$, $\CTB$, $\MT$, and $\CMA$ lacks some countable copowers. Consequently, neither is equivalent to a prevariety.
\end{theorem}

