In order to determine whether our numerical results converge to physical solutions, we performed a convergence test with three different resolutions, evaluating the evolution of the constraint violations induced by the truncation error of our numerical calculations. This test also represents a validation of our extensions made to the thorn used to evolve the 2-Proca stars. Therefore, we tracked those violations using the $L_2$ norm of the Hamiltonian constraint H (defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:constr:hamiltonian}), which is implemented in our code as $|H|_2:=(\;\sum^N_{i=0}H^2_i/N\;)^{1/2}$, with $N$ the total number of grid points.

Originally, we started our convergence test with the 3-refinement-level grid mentioned above, setting the low $m_V\{\Delta x, \Delta y, \Delta z \}=0.615$, medium $m_V\{\Delta x, \Delta y, \Delta z \}=0.410$, and high $m_V\{\Delta x, \Delta y, \Delta z \}=0.328$ resolutions of the finest (innermost) level. Figure~\ref{fig:convergence_test_A} shows the time evolutions of the minimum value of the lapse (top panel) and of $|H|_2$ (bottom panel) with these three resolutions for model $A$. The evolutions show that this configuration collapses and forms a BH for the three resolutions. The collapsing times are longer for finer resolutions. This is expected for systems perturbed by truncation errors because their impact on the evolution time scale is sensible to the grid resolution. Thus, at higher resolutions, the truncation errors are smaller and take more time to trigger the gravitational collapse. Despite this time difference, the minimum lapse evolution profiles are consistent between each other once a time shift is applied. On the other hand, the violation of the Hamiltonian constraint converges to zero by increasing the grid resolution with a convergence factor of about $3.5$, which is acceptable with the fourth-order accuracy of our evolution code. Notice that in order to see how the rescaled time profiles of $|H|_2$ overlay between each other, we applied the same time shift used for the minimum lapse profiles. 

