\subsection{Topological models}
(Co)homology of groups are related to (co)homology of their Eilenberg-MacLane spaces, possibly with respect to local coefficents. For simplicity, we make the statements for $\Z$-coefficients in this section. %Let us first review the concepts. 
\begin{definition}
Given a discrete group $G$, a cell complex $X$ is called an {\em Eilenberg-MacLane space  for $G$} or a {\em $K(G,1)$--cell complex} if $X$ is connected, $\pi_1X=G$, and $\pi_iX=0$ when $i\ge 2$. %In this case,  we also say that $X$ is a .
\end{definition}


The following theorem is well-known, see for example \cite[Section I.4 and III.1]{Bro82}. 
\begin{theorem}\label{THM: K(G,1)}%[\com{cite}]
Suppose $G$ is a discrete group. For     a $K(G,1)$--cell complex $X$,  there is a natural isomorphism $H_\ast(X;\Z)\cong H_\ast(G;\Z)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{COR: 3-mfd homology}
Let $M$ be a compact aspherical 3-manifold. Then there is an isomorphism 
