\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
%\item\label{rmk1} The $A$-polynomial is defined up to multiplicative units in $\C[M^{\pm},L^{\pm}]$. It is shown in \cite{CCGLS94} that a multiplicative  constant in $\C^\times$ can be chosen so that $A_K(M,L)$ has integral coefficients. We say that $A_K(M,L)$ is normalized if it belongs to $\Z[M,L]$, it  is not divisible by $M$ or $L$, and its coefficients have no common divisors. Then for normalized $A$-polynomials $A_J(M,L)$ and $A_K(M,L)$, $A_J\doteq A_K$ if and only if $A_J=\pm A_K$.  
