\section{Simplex case}

Note that the inequality in \eqref{eq: feas_space} can be written as
\begin{equation}
    [A B] \begin{bmatrix}p \\ \delta \end{bmatrix} \le b,
\end{equation}
which defines a (possibly unbounded) polyhedron $R$.
Projecting onto the disturbance space results in a polyhedral set of feasible $\delta$.
The ball $\Delta = \{\delta | \exists p \;\text{s.t.}\; Ap+B\delta+b\le 0\}$ must be contained in this polyhedron.
We may ask the broader question: for any $\delta$ in this set, is there an affine map such that $p_0+G\delta \in \mathcal{F}(\delta)$? (short answer: no)

\textbf{Claim}: 
Let $\{B\delta_j\}$ be a set of feasible disturbances.
Every convex combination of $\{B\delta_j\}$ is feasible (has a feasible $p$).

\begin{proof}
Let $B\delta = \sum_j w_jB\delta_j$ be a disturbance with convex coefficients $w$.
 For each $B\delta_j$ let $p_j \in\mathcal{F}(\delta_j)$.
Define $p^* = \sum_j w_j p_j$.
\begin{align}
    Ap_j + B\delta_j + b \le 0
    \\ Ap_jw_j + B\delta_jw_j + bw_j \le 0
    \\ \sum_j Ap_jw_j + B\delta_jw_j + bw_j \le 0
    \\  Ap^* + B\delta + b \le 0.
\end{align}
\end{proof}
Although this solution is guaranteed to exist, it cannot generally be represented as an affine transformation $p^* = p_0 + GB\delta$.

\textbf{Claim}: If the $\{B\delta_j\}$ form a simplex, a solution of the form $p^* = p_0 + G\delta$ exists.

\begin{proof}
Let $m$ be the dimension of the disturbances $B\delta$.
Consider $m+1$ feasible disturbances $B\delta_j$; i.e., for each $B\delta_j$ there is a vector $p_j \in \mathcal{F}(\delta_j)$.
We are looking for a unique convex combination - positive coefficients $w$ for a given $B\delta$ satisfying
\begin{align}
    \sum_j B\delta_j w_j &= B\delta \\
    \sum_j w_j &= 1 \\
    w_j \ge 0.
\end{align}
This can be written as 
\begin{equation}
    Dw = \begin{bmatrix} B\delta \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \quad w_j\ge 0,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
D = \begin{bmatrix}
    B\delta_1 & \ldots & B\delta_{m+1} \\
    1         & \ldots & 1 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}

Assuming no $\delta_j$ is a convex combination of the others, the matrix $D$ is full rank, hence invertible. 
Let $P = \begin{bmatrix} p_1 & \ldots & p_{m+1} \end{bmatrix}$
Then
\begin{equation}
    p = Pw = PD^{-1}\begin{bmatrix} B\delta \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = p_0 + G\delta.
\end{equation}

\end{proof}
\textcolor{cyan}{
Why did I use $B\delta$ instead of $\delta$? If $B$ is not square or not full rank, it might project onto a subspace.
It would be possible for $\{\delta\}$ to form a simplex but $\{B\delta\}$ not to, or vice versa.
If $B$ is full rank, its (pseudo)inverse can be used to reconstruct all the equations in terms of $\delta$.
}

\begin{remark}
    It is unlikely that the full feasible region $R = \begin{bmatrix} p\\\delta \end{bmatrix}$ is a simplex, or that the largest ball $\text{Ball}(\delta_0, \epsilon) \subset R$ is contained within a simplex having feasible extreme points.
    The region $R$ is a polytope that can be partitioned into simplex regions, so a piecewise linear map from $\delta$ to $p$ is possible using this result.
\end{remark}

Another way of constructing the unique convex combination $w$:

For each point $B\delta_j$, there is a unique $m-1$-dimensional hyperplane containing the remaining points.
Let $\hat{n}_j$ be the normal vector to this hyperplane (pointing inwards, towards $B\delta_j$).
Given an arbitrary $B\delta$ in the convex hull of $\{B\delta_j\}$, the coefficients
\begin{equation}\label{eq:simplex_combination}
    w_j = \frac{\hat{n}_j^T B\delta}{\hat{n}_j^T B\delta_j}
\end{equation}
form a convex combination of $B\delta_j$ for $B\delta$ that is unique.
For any $B\delta$ in the hyperplane spanned by $B\delta_i, i\ne j$, we must have $w_j = 0$, since $B\delta_j$ is not in the same hyperplane.
It follows that at $B\delta = B\delta_j$, $w_j = 1$, since $\sum_j w_j = 1$ for a convex combination.
Eq.~\eqref{eq:simplex_combination} is a linear map satisfying these conditions.

