\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The study of the dynamics of a continuous observable and non-controllable three-dimensional symmetric piecewise linear system with three zones can be reduced to the study of the existence of limit cycles for the piecewise differential equation $x'=ax+(b-a)\mathop{\rm sat}(x)+\mu\sin t$, where $\operatorname{sat}$ stands for the normalized saturation function.

This paper proves that the number of limit cycles of the equation is finite independently of $a,b,\mu$. Moreover, it is proven that the maximum number of limit cycles is exactly one or three for certain values of the parameters.

Using Melnikov theory for a certain deformation of the equation, it is proven that for small values of the perturbation parameter, there are exactly three, five or one limit cycles, depending on the values of $\mu$. This strengthens the conjecture that the equation has at most five limit cycles.
\end{abstract}

