    \begin{example}
		Consider the handlebody for a manifold $R_n$ shown on the left in Figure \ref{fig:Rn_1}. Initially, it's not clear if $R_n$ is in fact a closed $4$--manifold, i.e. that its 2--skeleton consisting of one $0$--, one $1$-- and one $2$--handle as in this diagram can be capped with one $3$--handle and one $4$--handle. We claim that $R_n$ is a closed $4$--manifold, and we use Proposition \ref{prop:dble_cover_alg} to determine its orientation double cover. Indeed, the Kirby calculus in Figure \ref{fig:Rn_2} shows that the orientation-double-cover of the 2--skeleton $R_n^{(2)}$ is the twist-spun lens space $L_n'$ with a copy of $S^1\times D^3$ removed. The handle moves involved are as follows. First, cancel the right-hand $0$--handle with the green $1$--handle to obtain (2). When this happens, the part of the diagram drawn on the canceled $0$--handle reverses orientation. With this, the entire right-hand side of the diagram is vertically reflected and the $2$--handle framing coefficients swap signs. After this, there are no longer twisted $1$--handles, so we may assign $2$--handle framing coefficients to entire links rather than arcs. Next slide the black $2$--handle over the blue one to obtain (3). The black $2$--handle may then be isotoped into an apparent meridian as in (4).

		Set $Y_n=\partial R_n^{(2)}$, which is a connected 3-manifold double covered by $S^1\times S^2$. This can be confirmed through nonorientable Kirby calculus for this boundary $3$--manifold or by the following calculation, relying on the classification of $3$--manifolds. Let $p\in Y_n$ be the basepoint shown on the right in Figure \ref{fig:Rn_2}. The orientation--reversing involution inducing the covering $L_n'\setminus (S^1\times D^3) \to R_n^{(2)}$ restricts to boundary, so $Y_n$ is nonorientable. Moreover, $\pi_1(Y_n, p)$ admits an infinite--cyclic index two subgroup, so is itself infinite. We claim that $Y_n \cong S^1\twist S^2$, which confirms the complete  Kirby diagram shown for $R_n$. To see this, it suffices to show that $\pi_1(Y_n)$ is cyclic. Once we've shown this, the classification of $3$--manifolds with $\pi_1\cong\Z$ completes the proof that $Y_n\cong S^1\twist S^2$ (e.g. by \cite{Kawauchi}, now with Perelman's solution of the $3$--dimensional Poincar\'{e} conjecture). 

		A presentation for $\pi_1(Y_n,p)$ can be given in a similar fashion to how the Wirtinger presentation gives a presentation for the fundamental group of a link complement. The generators for $\pi_1(Y_n,p)$ are either meridians to strands of the $2$--handle in $R_n$, or the loop $\ell$ which goes once through the twisted $1$--handle. Let $\mu$ be one of the meridian generators. Let $\gamma$ be a push-off of the framed $2$--handle attaching circle in $R_n$, which is null-homotopic in $Y_n$. The right of Figure \ref{fig:Rn_2} shows $\mu$, $\gamma$, and $\ell$.  The composition $\mu \ast \gamma \simeq \mu$ is homotopic to $\ell^{2n}$, and so $\pi_1(Y,p)$ is generated by $\ell$. This completes the proof that $Y_n\cong S^1\twist S^2$, and $R_n$ is a closed nonorientable $4$--manifold whose orientation double cover is $L_n'$.\footnote{The nonorientable $4$--manifold $R_n$ represents $N(2k,1,1)$ studied in \cite{teichner-etal}, and the Kirby diagram we present here confirms that these $4$--manifolds, generalizing the case of $X_1 = \mathbb{RP}^4$, admit perfect Morse functions, answering a question communicated to us by Peter Teichner.}


